// ===========================================================================
// 11. THE WAVE DIRECTOR  (GDD §2.6 + §8)
// ===========================================================================
// budget(tier, wave) = 0 + 36*tier + 96*wave, wave in 0..2, tier the 0..5
// SHADE TIER INDEX.  Read at tier 1, that is 36 + 96*wave  ->  36 / 132 / 228.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §8's TWO ANCHORS CONTRADICT EACH OTHER.  THE BODY COUNT WINS.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// GAME_DESIGN §8, 0:36-0:55, states BOTH of these about the same wave:
//
//      "Wave 1, tier 1, budget 60"        <- an accounting number
//      "~4 Husks and 6 Wisps"             <- what the player actually sees
//
// At GDD §2.2's own spawn-cost column (Wisp 2, Mote 3, Husk 6) those two are
// not the same wave.  4 Husks + 6 Wisps is 4*6 + 6*2 = 36 POINTS AND 10 BODIES.
// 60 points against tier 1's mean pool cost of 3.35 is 17.9 BODIES — nearly
// double, and the difference is not cosmetic:
//
//   MEASURED, `tools/probe_waveone.hml`, a bot that aims in 3-D, kites, uses
//   iron sights, reloads on the gap and never leaves the lantern's supply
//   radius:  0 of 12 first minutes cleared wave 1 at 60 points.  The director
//   pins at alive = 14 of 14 and stays there.  The reason is the ammunition
//   economy, not the aim: the opening loadout is 6 + 12 rounds and the lit
//   lantern resupplies 1 round / 0.8 s = 1.25 rounds/s, while 17.9 bodies
//   arriving at a 1.15 s cadence demand ~1.09 rounds/s sustained at point
//   blank and MORE past 14 m, where the Kestrel's 45 damage stops being a
//   two-shot Husk.  The player never gets ahead, so every §8 beat downstream
//   of the clear — WAVE 1 CLEARED, the path of lit lanterns, Odo, the village —
//   never arrives.  At 36 points the same bot clears 12 of 12.
//
// So the budget follows the body count, and the body count is §8's own
// illustration read at §2.2's own costs.  `probe_waveone.hml` pins BOTH halves
// with literals — `director_budget(1,0) == 36` AND
// `4*director_cost(HUSK) + 6*director_cost(WISP) == 36` — so the pair cannot
// drift apart again the way "budget 60" did while it sat alone.
//
// WHY NOT RAISE THE COSTS INSTEAD.  The other way to make 60 points buy ten
// bodies is to scale the six per-body costs by 1.79x (Wisp 2->4, Husk 6->9,
// which lands 4H + 6W on exactly 60).  It was priced and REJECTED:
//   * §2.2's cost column is the one GDD table with three consistent dependents
//     — §2.6's cadence, its alive cap and its hold duration all cohere at
//     ~110 bodies through it.  §8's illustration is the outlier, not the table.
//   * the costs are duplicated: `g_dir_cost` in `sim/director.hml` and
//     `g_ai_cost` in `sim/ai.hml`, cross-asserted by `probe_director.hml`.
//     `ai.hml` is not this task's file, so changing them means shipping a red
//     probe and a hand-off.  Retuning the budget needs neither.
// The exact alternative numbers are in the task report so the choice stays
// available; nothing here forecloses it.
//
// WHY THE BASE IS 0.  The GDD writes `60 + 45*tier` with a 1-based tier, i.e.
// an affine ladder with a floor.  A floor is wrong for THIS tier index: tier 0
// is a lit lantern's radius, and tier 0 means the dark does not come at all
// (`director_begin` refuses it outright).  A budget formula whose tier-0 cell
// is a non-zero number of points is a formula that disagrees with the state
// machine, and the previous BASE = 15 was exactly that.  0 + 36*tier keeps the
// ladder strictly increasing, makes the tier-0 cell honestly empty, and reads
// as "a tier-t hold opens with t tutorial waves' worth".
export let g_DIR_BUDGET_BASE: i32 = 0;
export let g_DIR_BUDGET_PER_TIER: i32 = 36;
// 72 -> 96.  Wave 1 shrank from 60 points to 36 and the hold must not shrink
// with it — this is the "keep the density" half of the decision below.  The 24
// points that left wave 1 are put back where escalation belongs, at the END of
// the hold: tier 1 now reads 36 / 132 / 228 (10.7 / 39.4 / 68.1 bodies) for the
// same 118-body total the 2:40 target was measured against.  The on-ramp got
// smaller; the night did not.
export let g_DIR_BUDGET_PER_WAVE: i32 = 96;
// THE CROWD BOUND OF THE NIGHT, AND THE ON-RAMP'S RELATION TO IT.
// 14 is a §2.6 anchor and `probe_director` pins it with a literal. What is NOT
// in the GDD, and is the thing that broke the first minute, is how wave 1's
// BODY COUNT sits against it. Measured over 20 000 replays of the director's own
// spend loop (`director_roll_kind` at the shipped costs and the tier-1 table):
//
//      tier 1 wave 1, 36 pts:  mean 11.0 bodies, min 6, max 17, 98.7 % <= 13
//      tier 2 wave 1, 72 pts:  ~15 bodies  -> MORE THAN THE CAP CAN HOLD
//
// So at tier 1 the on-ramp cannot fill the cap in 98.7 % of holds and does not
// in practice (`probe_waveone`: peak alive 7-11 of 14 across seven tier-1 first
// minutes). At tier 2 it fills it every time, and GAME_DESIGN §8's scripted
// first minute is NOT PINNED TO TIER 1 — `worldgen_shade_tier` decides it from
// the spawn chunk's jitter hash plus the night bonus, and it came up tier 2 on
// 5 of 12 seeds. Every one of those five failed to clear; six of the seven
// tier-1 seeds cleared. THE FIX BELONGS IN THE OPENING SCRIPT (force tier 1 for
// the scripted minute, the way it forces every other §8 beat), not here:
// flattening the budget ladder's first wave to keep tier 5's wave 1 under the
// cap would delete the escalation the ladder exists for, and would break
// `probe_director` §F, which asserts the cap DOES bind at tier 2.
export let g_DIR_ALIVE_CAP: i32 = 14;
export let g_DIR_ONSCREEN_CAP: i32 = 8;
export let g_DIR_SPAWN_MIN_M: f64 = 24.0;
export let g_DIR_SPAWN_MAX_M: f64 = 40.0;
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE NIGHT HOLD IS 2:40, NOT THE GDD'S 3:20.  RATIFIED, MEASURED, 2026-07-29.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// GDD §2.6 closes with "Hold duration ≈ 3:20 (3 waves + gaps)".  THAT NUMBER IS
// NOT REACHABLE FROM THE GDD'S OWN DIRECTOR PARAMETERS and never was:
//
//     tier-1 budget 369 pts / 3.35 mean pool cost = 110 bodies
//     110 bodies x 1.2 s cadence = 132 s + 2 x 8 s of wave gaps = 148 s FLOOR
//     measured against the reference player: 157.7 s   (target 200 s)
//
// The floor is the shortest a hold can be *if the player deletes every body the
// instant it arrives*, so 3:20 was never inside the reachable band at the
// director's own numbers.  This is the same class of internal inconsistency as
// the GDD's cumulative XP column (W4-7 found it; the gate confirmed it wrong).
//
// THE ONE-CONSTANT FIX WAS THE WRONG TRADE.  Cadence 1.56 s does land 201.1 s,
// exactly on the GDD's target — by RELEASING BODIES MORE SLOWLY.  Measured, it
// drops peak-alive to 12 of the 14-body cap, so the cap never binds and the
// density the cap exists to bound is never reached.  Longer AND thinner.  A
// night hold whose crowding limit is never touched is not a night hold.
//
// THE DECISION (the owner's): KEEP THE DENSITY, ACCEPT ~2:40.
// Retuned as one pass over cadence + budget together, tier 1, 12 holds per
// point, against probe_director's reference player (whose only free parameter,
// `acq`, is the seconds of per-body engagement overhead — see that file):
//
//                       cadence  budget(1,·)   bodies  hold @acq 0.35   peak alive
//   before               1.20 s  105/123/141      110      157.7 s        8 @acq 1.2
//   rejected (thinner)   1.56 s  105/123/141      110      201.1 s       12 (cap never binds)
//   SHIPPED              1.15 s   60/132/204      118      162.3 s       13 @acq 1.2, 14 @1.6
//
// So the shipped hold is 2:42 at the reference player, 4 % denser in raw spawn
// rate than before, and MUCH denser late: peak-alive at a 1.2 s engagement
// overhead goes 8 -> 13 of 14, because the escalated wave 3 (204 pts, ~61
// bodies back to back) is where the crowd now happens.  The alive cap still
// binds hard from acq 1.6 s upward, in 12 of 12 holds.
//
// THE CEILING NOBODY WROTE DOWN: NIGHT IS 300 s (g_PHASE_DUSK_END -> 1.0 of a
// 960 s day).  `director_step` ABORTS a live hold at dawn, so total bodies are
// bounded by what the slowest survivable player can clear inside 300 s, not by
// taste.  MEASURED at 118 bodies: acq 2.0 s finishes at 273.9 s mean / 287.0 s
// worst — inside 300.  At 125 bodies (cadence 1.05 s, per-wave 80) holds began
// being aborted by dawn.  118 IS NEAR THAT CEILING.  If you raise the budget
// again, re-measure the acq 2.0 s column before you believe it.
//
// COSTS WERE NOT TOUCHED and could not be from here: the six per-body spawn
// costs live in `sim/director.hml` (mirrored by `ai_cost_of` in `sim/ai.hml`),
// neither of which this task owns.  They are the remaining lever, and the one
// that would close §8's "~4 Husks and 6 Wisps" body count as well.
export let g_DIR_SPAWN_CADENCE_S: f64 = 1.15;
export let g_DIR_WAVE_GAP_S: f64 = 8.0;
export let g_DIR_WAVES_PER_HOLD: i32 = 3;
export let g_DIR_SPAWN_BEHIND_BIAS: f64 = 0.70;      // fraction spawned behind view

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE RAMP — how fast the dark is allowed to deepen.  MEASURED 2026-07-31.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// These two numbers exist because a 45-minute session measured THREE nights at
// director tier 1 and spawned 346 bodies drawn from three classes:
//
//     W 127  M 132  H 87   S 0   A 0   B 0
//
// Spitter, Warden and Bulwark shipped ZERO times in 45 minutes, because tier
// 1's composition row is the only row that contains none of them and the hold
// never left tier 1.  `sim/director.hml`'s DESIGN 7 has the full diagnosis.
//
// `hold_tier = min(night_tier_at_the_anchor, BASE + PER_HOLD * holds_done)`
//
//   BASE = 1      night one is a tutorial and a tutorial that kills you is not
//                 one.  This SUBSUMES `sim.hml`'s own "pin the first hold to
//                 tier 1" clamp — the two agree exactly at holds_done == 0 and
//                 neither depends on the other being present.
//   PER_HOLD = 1  one tier per night SURVIVED.  Not per night STARTED: the
//                 counter is `holds_done`, which only moves when all three
//                 waves clear (`director_step`'s DIR_CLEARING branch).  A night
//                 dawn aborts teaches nothing and advances nothing.
//
// WHY min() AND NOT +.  A bonus would let a player standing on one square
// escalate forever; a CEILING makes the world's own distance gradient
// (`worldgen_shade_tier` = floor(d/200) + jitter + phase) the thing that
// decides how deep a night can get, and the ramp only decides how fast you are
// allowed to approach it.  Measured at seed 1337's spawn post: the post sits in
// the ONLY tier-2 cell of its 5x5 chunk neighbourhood, and tier 3 is 30 m away,
// tier 4 is 32 m away and tier 5 is 162 m away.  That is the reason to walk.
//
// WHY PER_HOLD IS NOT 2.  I ASSUMED IT WOULD MAKE NO DIFFERENCE — the world's
// ceiling binds at the spawn post either way — AND I WAS WRONG, so here is the
// measurement instead of the assumption.  Five seeds, 35-minute sessions, the
// shipping `demobot.hml` driver, three nights each:
//
//                night tiers per seed          player deaths (mean)  kills (mean)
//   PER_HOLD 1   122 122 122 122 122           45.6  (23..68)         201.0
//   PER_HOLD 2   122 133 133 133 132           76.4  (44..139)        172.2
//
// At 2 the second night jumps straight to tier 3 on four of the five seeds —
// the Warden's first night is also the player's second — and deaths rise 68 %
// while kills FALL 14 %, which is the signature of a player who is dying rather
// than fighting.  The ramp is the guard rail; the world's gradient is the
// content.  1 keeps both; 2 removes the guard rail and buys one extra tier that
// the player was going to reach anyway by walking 30 m.
export let g_DIR_RAMP_BASE: i32     = 1;
export let g_DIR_RAMP_PER_HOLD: i32 = 1;

